Feasibility: HIGH — architectural note only, no code written this pass.

This is a *discovery* feature, so it belongs in lexphon/discovery/ as a third
source alongside vital.py and popular.py — e.g. discovery/corpus_ner.py exposing
a function that returns a list[str] of candidate titles. Those candidates then
flow through the existing dedupe() -> fetch_leads() -> extract_pronunciations()
pipeline unchanged, so it composes cleanly with the phase-1 CLI (a new
`--ner-text FILE` / `--ner-corpus` flag would sit next to `--vital-level` and
`--popular-limit`).

Key architectural points / gotchas:

1. spaCy yields *surface mentions*, not canonical Wikipedia titles. "Paris" or
   "Robespierre" must be resolved to a single article title before the fetch, or
   the fetch 404s. Recommend action=query&list=search (or a Wikidata search) to
   normalize each mention. Multi-word PERSON/GPE mentions are the highest-value
   case; casing must be preserved (Wikipedia titles are title-cased, NER gives
   the source text's casing).

2. Where does the NER input text come from? Two options:
   - (Recommended, cheap) Run NER over the lead sections the phase-1 pipeline
     already fetches — reuse the fetch and discover *secondary* entities.
   - (Heavier) A dedicated corpus / Wikipedia dump. Anything >2 GB needs explicit
     user permission per AGENTS.md, so lead-reuse is the sensible default.

3. "Filter with an ordinary dictionary" removes NEs that are really common words
   so TTS doesn't need them. Pitfall: case-insensitive membership is wrong —
   "August", "May", "Turkey" vs "turkey" are real targets that a lowercase
   lookup would wrongly drop. Recommend: only drop a mention when its lowercase
   form is a common-English word AND the surface form is not title-cased, or lean
   on the NER labels (PERSON/GPE/LOC are strong signals) and only
   dictionary-filter ORG. Data: a small pinned wordlist (e.g. wordfreq, or a
   plain wordlist file in resources/); avoid /usr/share/dict/words (not portable).

4. Dependency note: spaCy + en_core_web_sm (~15 MB) must land in requirements.txt
   (or an optional extra), and the model install is `python -m spacy download
   en_core_web_sm`, not plain pip — that complicates reproducible installs.
   Decide whether this is a hard dep or an optional[ner] extra so the core CLI
   stays dependency-light.

5. Minor spec mismatch to confirm: the prose lists PERSON/GPE/LOC but the example
   also returns ORG ("Jacobin Club"). ORG is the lowest-value label for
   pronunciation (org names are usually ordinary words); decide whether to keep it.

